description:
Motivation: mixed-length production traffic exhibits bimodal sequence lengths (short chat turns and long context retrievals). Existing single-path FlashAttention variants either optimized for short sequences (low overhead) or long sequences (amortized kernel efficiency), but switching between them was coarse and expensive. This PR implements a heterogeneous FlashFusion strategy plus two orthogonal runtime improvements: a rotary offset compression scheme to shrink KV checkpoints, and a KV slab allocator to reduce allocation latency and memory fragmentation. Together these reduce p50/p95 latency and improve throughput for mixed workloads.

What this PR does (single continuous description): Implement a heterogeneous kernel dispatcher that selects one of three attention execution paths at request time: (1) tiny-fastpath: low-overhead kernel optimized for seq <= 64 and heads <= 16, (2) flashfusion-core: vectorized FlashFusion kernel for medium sequences with fused rotary and qkv reductions, (3) windowed-chunk-path: tiled kernel for very long sequences which streams KV blocks with prefetch hints. The dispatcher integrates with the existing continuous-batcher so requests are routed to compatible kernels in the same batch; when multiple kernels are required in a batch the scheduler shards the batch and issues parallel kernel launches to minimize stall.

Rotary offset compression: introduce a lightweight delta-encoding for rotary embeddings stored in the KV cache. Instead of keeping full 32/16-bit rotary offsets per token, we store block-level base offsets plus per-token 8-bit deltas when possible. For long runs the compressor falls back to explicit offsets. This reduces KV metadata by ~40% on retrieval-heavy workloads and reduces memcpy cost at eviction/restore.

KV slab allocator: replace many per-request allocations with pre-partitioned slabs per-device and per-quantization profile. The allocator supports fast O(1) alloc/free for KV blocks and background compaction to coalesce free slabs without blocking the fast path. The allocator also exports debug counters for fragmentation and high-water marks.

Scheduler improvements: the continuous-batcher receives kernel-compatible hints from the dispatcher (preferred kernel id, estimate of kv size) and applies two heuristics: (a) auto-split large batches that contain a mix of short and long sequences, launching a short-fastpath sub-batch immediately and streaming the long sub-batch; (b) preferred affinity for warm KV blocks to reduce cross-stream memory pressure. We also added an opt-in flag runtime.hetero_flashfusion.enabled to enable gradual rollout.

Testing & benchmarks: unit tests for delta encoding, slab allocator stress test, end-to-end integration on the bench cluster. Microbenchmarks recorded: mixed chat+retrieval synthetic workload (50% seq=32, 50% seq=1024) shows p50 latency -18%, p95 -12% and overall tokens/sec +24% on an A100 80GB node compared to baseline flash2 kernel. KV memory metadata reduced ~38% and average allocation latency improved from 85us to 12us under synthetic pressure.

Backward compatibility & rollout: feature gated behind runtime flag and AUTO_ROLLOUT=canary. The dispatcher falls back to the legacy flash2 kernel if a requested kernel path fails. No public API changes.

Checklist:
- [x] Heterogeneous dispatcher + kernel selection logic
- [x] FlashFusion core kernel (CUDA) with fused rotary reduction
- [x] Rotary offset compressor + unit tests
- [x] KV slab allocator implementation + stress tests
- [x] Scheduler integration with continuous-batcher and auto-split heuristics
- [x] Benchmarks and perf dashboards updated
- [x] Docs (runtime flags, observability counters)

Risks & mitigations: the new allocator alters device memory accounting; we added careful high-water metrics and a safety path that returns to the legacy allocator on OOM. Kernel correctness validated against the reference attention with exhaustive random seeds and fp16/fp32 comparisons.

Related: builds on prior work in this area (see ENG-1724 for flash fusion experiments) and touches runtime, kernels, and observability surfaces.

Representative diff excerpt (illustrative, not full patch) included inline to describe the dispatcher hook: 

--- a/src/runtime/scheduler.cc
+++ b/src/runtime/scheduler.cc
@@ -412,6 +412,28 @@ void ContinuousBatcher::dispatchBatch(Batch& b) {
+  // compute preferred kernel for each request and group
+  for (auto &r : b.requests) {
+    r.meta.preferred_kernel = KernelSelector::pick(r);
+  }
+  auto groups = group_by(b.requests, [](const Request& r){ return r.meta.preferred_kernel; });
+  for (auto &g : groups) {
+    if (g.key == KernelId::TinyFastpath) {
+      launchTinyPath(g.requests);
+    } else if (g.key == KernelId::FlashFusionCore) {
+      launchFlashFusion(g.requests);
+    } else {
+      launchWindowedChunked(g.requests);
+    }
+  }
 }

